  "result": "I added the `refunded` state, and the full test suite passes: 13 tests, 0 failures (`python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -t .`). Nothing is committed.\n\n**What changed**\n- **`shop/orders.py`:** `refunded` is now one of the order states. Paid and shipped orders can move to it, and it has no way out. New and cancelled orders can't be refunded.\n- **`shop/reports.py`:** no change was needed. `sales_by_customer` only counts paid and shipped orders, so refunded orders are already left out of the totals.\n- **Tests:**\n  - `tests/test_orders.py` now checks the two allowed moves (paid\u2192refunded, shipped\u2192refunded) and the blocked ones (new\u2192refunded, cancelled\u2192refunded, refunded\u2192anything).\n  - The new file `tests/test_reports.py` checks that refunded orders don't count in `sales_by_customer`. It covers a customer with a mix of paid, shipped and refunded orders, and a customer whose only orders are refunded.\n\nI searched the rest of the code for places that list order states. Only these two files do: there's no database schema or migration, and saving an order just writes the state as plain text.",
